            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Sarah Jo"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used EVOO instead of corn oil and I cut back just a touch on the salt. The recipe didn't state what kind of onion to use, I made a judgement call and used an extra large sweet onion. That was the only change I made in this recipe. We had these piled on top of barbequed kielbasa. NO leftovers. This was absolutely fantastic."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mike Moran"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Absolutely on the $ (less the cayenne!). While experimenting I sweated the onions, added a touch of smoked paprika and Peter Lugers steak sauce. Killer!",
            "datePublished": "2020-10-28T21:10:28.120Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ALESANDRINA"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I’m from NYC and grew up getting franks from the carts. This is the perfect recipe and brings me back to my childhood. I increased the recipe using 6 lbs of Vidalia onions for a party and had to tweak the amount of ingredients a bit. I could not justify multiplying the ingredients by 10!!! My Italian hotdog vendor added garlic powder, so did I. About two tablespoons for 6 lbs of onions. The onions came out perfect.",
            "datePublished": "2024-05-25T15:52:24.788Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "KeenBran9499"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very good starting recipe. I added just a few things. First, a bit more on the hot sauce and cayenne, but that’s because I like it very hot. I added about 1.5 tsp’s of smoked paprika and 1 tsp garlic powder. Next time, I’m going to add just a hint of distilled white vinegar.",
            "datePublished": "2024-11-05T22:27:42.037Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jennifer"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Made it for hubby, who loves onion sauce on his hot dogs. This was great! A tad spicy for our family, but easily resolved by doubling the onions, adding a touch more sugar and water, then just cooking it down more. My hubs &amp; sons kept repeating how good it was. Thank you for a great recipe. Now I don't need to ever buy the jarred sauce again. :-)",
            "datePublished": "2016-03-06T05:03:53.850Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Laura L"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was delicious! I left out the cayenne pepper because I was afraid it would be too spicy. It was spicy even without it, but tolerable for me (a proclaimed spice wimp). Really tasty though - we didn't have tomato paste, so we used condensed tomato soup and it seemed to work out just as well! Thanks!",
            "datePublished": "2017-09-04T22:57:33.033Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Nora"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Okay my guess is that this recipe gets 5 stars but did not use Frank's Hot sauce, I used another and it was too hot. I am posting so that others don't make the same mistake I did. Frank's Hot sauce has a SHU (heat) rating of only 450 while Tabasco scores 700. Dave's Insanity Sauce score 175,000 and the list I found on line goes to 16,000,000! I will be modifying my first batch and will make again. LOVE this type of sauce! Thank you for posting.",
            "datePublished": "2017-07-26T21:02:15.413Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jason"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I live in Asia and miss being able to buy a jar of onions from Sabretts, which brings me back to my childhood days in NYC. Being able to make this at home now is a gamechanger. I made one substitute as I didn't find cayenne pepper where I shop, but used cajun pepper which was available. This is a great recipe!",
            "datePublished": "2026-05-08T11:35:12.333Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Paul"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I've made these onions twice and they are very good. Even my wife, who has a bit of an aversion to onions, found them good. I don't feel the cayenne pepper is needed, so I left it out the second time around; my chilli powder and the hot sauce provided enough warmth, I think.",
            "datePublished": "2025-10-17T02:13:31.123Z"
          },
